# Lab gateway. Terminates TLS for internal service names and proxies to the
# services behind it. See docs/gateway.md.
#
# Certificates are real Let's Encrypt certificates obtained with the DNS-01
# challenge against the labxp.io zone. DNS-01 proves control of a name by
# writing a TXT record rather than by answering an inbound request, which is
# why every name below works in a browser with a normal padlock while the lab
# accepts no inbound connection from the internet and no internal CA root is
# installed on any device.
#
# TO ADD A SERVICE: copy a block, point it at the upstream, add the matching
# A record to BOTH Pi-holes (192.168.10.11 and .12) pointing at
# 192.168.201.14, and open the firewall from pfe to the upstream. Then push --
# gateway-sync picks it up within the minute. No Terraform, no VM rebuild.
{
	# Recommended, currently unset: an ACME account with no contact address
	# gets no expiry or account warnings from Let's Encrypt. Add one line:
	#   email you@example.com
	# Left out rather than guessed; a wrong address is worse than none.

	# Staging CA for testing. Let's Encrypt allows 5 duplicate certificates
	# per week, and a loop that keeps failing after issuance burns that fast.
	# Uncomment while debugging issuance, then remove -- staging certs are
	# not trusted by browsers.
	# acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
}

# Shared TLS configuration. Every site below imports it.
(tls_cloudflare) {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CF_DNS_API_TOKEN}

		# Pin propagation checks to public resolvers. Caddy confirms its
		# _acme-challenge TXT record is visible before asking Let's Encrypt
		# to validate; left on the system resolver that check would go to
		# Pi-hole, which holds local overrides for these very names, and a
		# split-horizon answer makes the check fail or hang.
		resolvers 1.1.1.1 9.9.9.9
	}
}

# --- The portal -------------------------------------------------------------
# The apex, not a subdomain. That is a deliberate choice with one consequence
# worth knowing: both Pi-holes answer labxp.io with this host's address, so
# anything published at the apex on the public internet is unreachable from
# inside the lab. Subdomains are unaffected -- cmd.labxp.io still resolves
# publicly -- PROVIDED the Pi-hole entries are plain host records for the
# exact name. See gateway/README.md; a dnsmasq `address=/labxp.io/...` line
# would match every subdomain and take cmd.labxp.io down with it.
labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy homepage:3000
}

# --- Private frontends ------------------------------------------------------
redlib.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy redlib:8080
}

plex.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	# Plex speaks plain HTTP on 32400 and redirects / to /web itself.
	# Websockets (used by the dashboard and playback state) need no special
	# handling -- Caddy proxies them transparently.
	reverse_proxy 192.168.10.10:32400
}

# --- Infrastructure ---------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT pve.labxp.io. That name is the Proxmox API endpoint the
# bpg/proxmox provider talks to from CI; repointing it would put every
# Terraform run in this repository through Caddy, and a proxy hiccup would
# become a CI outage across every deployment. This is a second name for
# browser access only.
#
# Proxmox serves its own self-signed certificate on 8006, so the hop from
# Caddy to it cannot be verified. That hop stays inside the lab; the hop the
# browser makes is the one that is properly secured.
proxmox.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy https://pve.labxp.io:8006 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}

# Pi-hole's UI lives under /admin and its root serves a blocked-domain page,
# so send the bare hostname there rather than making everyone remember the
# path. Both are proxied separately on purpose: reaching one when the other
# is rebooting is the entire reason there are two.
#
# HTTPS upstream, not plain HTTP. Pi-hole serves TLS itself and answers :80
# with a 308 to the HTTPS URL, so proxying to :80 produced a redirect loop
# dressed up as a working page -- Caddy returned the 308 to the browser,
# which came back to Caddy, which asked Pi-hole again.
#
# The certificate it presents is self-signed and carries its own name, not
# dns01.labxp.io, so this hop cannot be verified and skipping verification is
# the only option short of trusting Pi-hole's CA on the gateway. That hop
# stays inside the lab between two hosts it owns; the hop the browser makes,
# which is the one worth protecting, is properly secured.
dns01.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	redir / /admin
	reverse_proxy https://192.168.10.11 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}

dns02.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	redir / /admin
	reverse_proxy https://192.168.10.12 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}
